2. Related Work

2.1 Existential Risk and the Backup Argument

The ethical and strategic imperative to reduce existential risk has been rigorously formulated by Bostrom [1] and quantified by Ord [2], who estimates the per‑century probability of an existential catastrophe at roughly one in six. Within this discourse, multi‑planetary settlement is promoted as a risk diversification strategy. Gottlieb [5] and analyses in the space‑policy literature [3,4] caution, however, that a colony that remains fundamentally dependent on Earth offers only a marginal decrease in extinction probability. Our work directly builds on this caution by providing the mathematical machinery to measure exactly how much risk is reduced as a function of dependency structure, thus translating an informal critique into a quantitative science.

2.2 Interdependent Network Cascades

The seminal work of Buldyrev et al. [6] demonstrated that when two networks are coupled by dependency links, the failure of even a small fraction of nodes in one network can trigger a cascade that fragments the entire system in a discontinuous (first‑order) percolation transition, in stark contrast to the continuous transition of a single network. This result has been extended to networks‑of‑networks with arbitrary topologies [7], spatially embedded dependencies [8], and scenarios where inner‑ and inter‑dependency links coexist, giving rise to tricritical points [9]. These models provide the formal engine for RDT’s Section 7: the Earth‑Mars system is precisely a pair of interdependent functional networks, and the transition to resilience is identified with the point at which the Mars network no longer contains a giant vulnerable component dependent on Earth.

2.3 Common‑Cause Failures and Copulas

In nuclear power plant probabilistic risk assessment, the beta‑factor model captures the fraction of component failures that are due to a shared cause [10,11]. This formalism maps directly onto our correlation coefficient: two civilizational branches suffer a common‑cause failure when a single hazard (e.g., a destabilizing AI breakthrough) disables both. Because existential catastrophes are extreme tail events, simple Pearson correlation is inadequate; copula theory, particularly the t‑copula with its tail‑dependence parameter, has been applied to model correlated rare failures in reliability engineering [12,13]. We adapt the Extreme CoVaR index [13] to define a systemic extinction correlation.

2.4 Quantitative Independence Metrics for Space Settlement

NASA’s strategic planning distinguishes “Earth‑Reliant” from “Earth‑Independent” operations and specifies progressive closure of resource loops [14]. Rollock and Klaus [15] decompose self‑sufficiency into self‑awareness and functional closure. The Mars Risk Index (MRI) [16] provides a societal‑readiness metric using frequency–consequence curves, but focuses on Earth’s preparedness rather than the settlement’s intrinsic functional independence. Our index I(t) is distinct: it measures the probability that the Mars civilization survives permanent isolation, irrespective of Earth’s intentions or readiness. The bootstrapping model of Metzger et al. [17] quantifies the minimal landed mass required to achieve industrial closure, and Salotti [18] calculates minimal viable populations. We integrate these into the dynamic reduction of dependencies in Section 7.

