7. Dynamics of Dependency Reduction and the Autonomy Threshold

7.1 Technology Spiral and Bootstrapping

The evolution of the dependency matrix D(t) (where entries are the import fractions e_k(t)) is driven by investment in in‑situ resource utilization (ISRU) and capacity building. Building on Metzger et al. [17], we propose a logistic growth model for each domain’s closure:

\frac{dI_k}{dt} = r_k \, I_k (1 - \frac{I_k}{K_k}) \, (1 - \beta_{\text{eff}}),

where r_k is the learning rate, K_k the maximum achievable closure for domain k (which may be <1 due to fundamental constraints, e.g., rare‑earth elements), and \beta_{\text{eff}} is the effective coupling strength from the interdependent network. Early on, many domains are stuck at low I_k because they depend on Earth for critical subsystems; as these bottlenecks are removed, I(t) = \min_k I_k(t) jumps when the last weak link crosses a threshold.

7.2 Percolation Phase Transition and the Critical Coupling

From the perspective of network theory, the Mars settlement is a network of functional nodes linked to Earth via dependency edges. Following Buldyrev et al. [6], let q be the fraction of Mars nodes that are directly dependent on functioning Earth nodes. As local capabilities replace Earth dependencies, q decreases. There exists a critical coupling q_c below which the Mars network no longer contains a giant vulnerable component – i.e., after Earth‑nodes are removed, the largest connected cluster of functioning Mars nodes remains large enough to sustain civilization. For Erdős–Rényi interdependent networks with average degree \langle k \rangle,

q_c = 1 - \frac{1}{\kappa - 1},

where \kappa = \langle k^2 \rangle / \langle k \rangle [7]. For realistic civilizational networks, q_c can be estimated via simulation (Section 8). When q < q_c, the system undergoes a discontinuous transition from a state where Earth‑collapse annihilates Mars to one where Mars survives. This threshold defines the birth of an autonomous civilization – not a gradual accumulation of capabilities but a sharp phase change in the fragility landscape.

7.3 Reinforcement and Optimal Investment

Recent work [19] shows that reinforcing only a fraction \rho^* \approx 0.1756 of the most critical nodes in an interdependent network can switch the collapse from first‑order to second‑order, dramatically increasing robustness. This translates into a resource allocation strategy: prioritize the 17% of Mars subsystems that serve as linchpins (semiconductor fabrication, closed‑loop agriculture, autonomous medical supply) to achieve maximum decorrelation per unit cost.

