Appendix A. Notation and Basic Definitions

A.1. Civilizational network. A distributed civilization is represented by a multiplex network \mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}) where
\mathcal{V} = \mathcal{V}_E \cup \mathcal{V}_M \cup \dots are the functional nodes, partitioned by location (Earth E, Mars M, etc.).
Nodes represent societal functions drawn from a domain set \mathcal{D} = \{1,2,\dots,K\}, e.g., energy, food, manufacturing, etc.
Edges \mathcal{E} are directed dependency links: u \to v with weight w_{uv} \in [0,1] meaning that function v requires a fraction w_{uv} of its critical input from u to remain fully operational.

A.2. State variables. Each node i has a binary state x_i \in \{0,1\} (0 = failed) or a continuous vitality \phi_i \in [0,1]. For cascading failure models, we use a threshold rule: node i fails if the total weighted failure from its in-neighbors exceeds a robustness threshold \theta_i:

\phi_i(t+1) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } \sum_{j \to i} w_{ji} (1-\phi_j(t)) \ge \theta_i,\\
1 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}

A.3. Interplanetary dependency fraction. For Mars, the import fraction from Earth in domain k is e_k \in [0,1]. The total coupling parameter q (Section 7) is the fraction of Mars nodes that have at least one direct Earth dependency edge.

A.4. Catastrophe scenarios. A hazard h is a set of Earth nodes that are initially failed. The cascade propagates according to the threshold dynamics across the entire Earth-Mars network. Extinction occurs if, after cascade, all Mars nodes fail or the surviving component is too small to sustain civilization (defined by a size threshold S_{\text{crit}}).

