Appendix B. Leontief Closure and the Independence Index I(t)

B.1. Technology matrix for a single location. For Mars, consider an economy with n produced goods (domains). Let A = (a_{ij}) be the n\times n domestic input-output matrix, where a_{ij} is the amount of good i required to produce one unit of good j. Let f be the vector of final demand (consumption, investment). The standard Leontief equation is x = Ax + f, with solution x = (I - A)^{-1} f. Total domestic output x must meet final demand and intermediate use.

B.2. Import dependency. Some inputs cannot be produced domestically and must be imported from Earth. Let m be the vector of imports, each element m_i is the quantity of good i imported per unit of domestic output of good i. The import coefficient vector e is defined componentwise as e_i = m_i / x_i (the fraction of total supply of good i that is imported). The domestic production balance becomes x = Ax + f + m, and with import coefficients m = \operatorname{diag}(e) x, we have:

x = Ax + f + \operatorname{diag}(e) x \quad \Rightarrow \quad (I - A - \operatorname{diag}(e)) x = f.

The matrix L_{\text{open}} = (I - A - \operatorname{diag}(e))^{-1} gives the total domestic and import-embodied requirements.

B.3. Closure after isolation. After isolation, imports cease (e=0). The settlement must survive using only domestic capabilities. The post-isolation output must satisfy x^* = A x^* + f. The ability to meet final demand from domestic production alone is governed by whether the purely domestic Leontief inverse L = (I - A)^{-1} exists and yields non-negative output. The maximal final demand that can be sustained without imports is limited by the existing industrial capacity, which we assume is proportional to the pre-isolation installed capacity vector K. For simplicity, we define the domain closure as:

I_k = \min\left(1, \frac{\text{max domestic supply of good } k \text{ without imports}}{\text{required demand for good } k}\right).

Using the Leontief structure, a linear approximation of the capability to replace imports locally gives:

I_k = 1 - \sum_{j} L_{kj} \, e_j,

provided the sum is \le 1; otherwise I_k=0. This expression measures the fraction of total input requirements (direct and indirect) that are not reliant on Earth. I_k=1 means the entire supply chain can be sustained locally.

B.4. Aggregation. The global Independence Index is the bottleneck:

I(t) = \min_{k \in \mathcal{D}_{\text{crit}}} I_k(t),

where \mathcal{D}_{\text{crit}} includes all domains indispensable for survival (energy, food, water, oxygen, basic manufacturing, computing, medicine). The weakest-link principle ensures that a failure in any one domain implies civilizational collapse.

B.5. Relation to survival probability. In a stochastic setting, let the isolated settlement survive for a fixed horizon T if no domain fails. If domain failures are independent conditional on the closure values, then P_{\text{survive}} \approx \prod_k \Phi(I_k), where \Phi is a sigmoid function linking closure to reliability. A conservative calibration sets I(t) \equiv \hat{P}_{\text{survive}} directly.

