Appendix C. Common-Cause Beta and Copula Dependence

C.1. Beta-factor model per hazard. For a given existential hazard h, define the marginal probabilities p_E(h) = P(\text{Earth extinction}\mid h) and p_M(h) = P(\text{Mars extinction}\mid h). The joint extinction probability is p_{EM}(h). The common-cause beta is:

\beta_h = \frac{p_{EM}(h)}{p_E(h)}.

If failures are independent, p_{EM} = p_E p_M and \beta_h = p_M(h). If perfectly correlated, p_{EM} = p_E = p_M and \beta_h = 1. The beta-factor quantifies the fraction of Earth-failure events that also kill Mars.

C.2. Tail dependence via copula. For continuous severity measures (e.g., magnitude of AI disruption), let F_E, F_M be the marginal CDFs. A copula C couples them: F_{EM}(u,v) = C(F_E(u), F_M(v)). The upper tail dependence coefficient is:

\lambda_h = \lim_{t \to 1^-} P\left(F_M(X_M) > t \mid F_E(X_E) > t\right).

For a t-copula with correlation \rho and degrees of freedom \nu, \lambda = 2 t_{\nu+1}\left(-\sqrt{\frac{(\nu+1)(1-\rho)}{1+\rho}}\right), non-zero for \rho > -1. For a Gaussian copula, \lambda = 0 unless \rho=1, making it unsuitable for modeling joint existential catastrophes. We advocate t-copula as a minimal model capturing tail dependence.

C.3. Aggregating across hazards. The set of hazards \mathcal{H} is partitioned into classes with weights w_h from existential risk assessments (e.g., Ord’s risk estimates). The aggregate correlation index is:

C(E,M) = \sum_{h \in \mathcal{H}} w_h \,\beta_h \quad \text{or} \quad \sum_{h \in \mathcal{H}} w_h \,\lambda_h.

Because \beta_h or \lambda_h are dimensionless and comparable across hazards, this weighted average measures the overall coupling of catastrophe between Earth and Mars. A fully decorrelated backup would yield C(E,M) \approx 0.

---

Appendix D. Hazard Decorrelation Taxonomy and Formal Basis

D.1. Categorization criterion. For each hazard h, we define a decorrelation length-scale \ell_h: the physical or informational distance over which the correlation \beta_h drops by a factor of e. For kinetic hazards (asteroid, supervolcano), \ell_h is on the order of Earth’s radius, so interplanetary separation yields \beta_h \approx p_M \ll 1. For informational hazards (AI, software monoculture, fast-spreading pandemics), \ell_h is effectively infinite because the propagation medium (digital networks, biological vectors with short latency) connects Earth and Mars almost instantaneously. Thus, unless specific informational firewalls are erected, \beta_h remains close to 1.

D.2. Per-hazard beta estimation. For class-1 hazards (intrinsically correlated), we set a lower bound \beta_h \ge \beta_{\text{info}}, where \beta_{\text{info}} is determined by the fraction of Mars systems that share Earth’s software or biobasis. For class-3, \beta_h \le \beta_{\text{kinetic}} \ll 1. Class-2 hazards sit in between and depend on degree of institutional separation. A table of illustrative values is provided in the supplementary material.

D.3. Mathematical consequence. The aggregate C(E,M) decomposes as:

C(E,M) = \underbrace{\sum_{h\in \text{Class }1} w_h \beta_h}_{\text{undecorrelatable fraction}} + \underbrace{\sum_{h\in \text{Class }2} w_h \beta_h(\tau)}_{\text{partially decorrelatable}} + \underbrace{\sum_{h\in \text{Class }3} w_h \beta_h(0)}_{\text{well decorrelated}},

where \tau is a vector of effort variables (e.g., industrial duplication). Minimizing C(E,M) translates into allocating resources to reduce \beta_h(\tau) for class-2 hazards and to break informational coupling for class-1.

